I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: “The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.” The first reply is the exact text "```json\n{\"a\":1}\n```\n```json\n{\"b\":2} {\"c\":3}\n```". The second reply is the exact text "```json\n{\"a\":1}\n```". For the first reply, the rule requires the object {"c":3}, because the last object inside any code fence is {"c":3}. For the second reply, the rule requires {"a":1}. The check recover_json_object returns {"a":1} for both replies: in the first reply, the second fenced block’s whole content is not a single JSON object, so the implementation skips it and returns the first fenced block’s object. This would be shown false if recover_json_object returned {"c":3} for the first reply, or if the documented rule were read as requiring the whole fenced block to be a single object rather than each top-level object inside it.